[\n  {\n    \"title\": \"Tycoon2FA hijacks Microsoft 365 accounts via device-code phishing\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-17T10:43:00-04:00\",\n    \"summary\": \"Researchers report that the Tycoon2FA phishing kit has added OAuth device-code phishing, abusing legitimate Microsoft device login flows and (in observed chains) Trustifi click-tracking redirects to trick victims into authorizing attacker-controlled devices. Once victims enter the device code on microsoft.com/devicelogin and complete MFA, attackers receive OAuth tokens enabling account takeover without stealing passwords in the traditional sense.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Enables rapid Microsoft 365 account takeover with MFA bypass-by-design via token issuance. Compromised mailboxes and cloud files can be used for BEC, data theft, and lateral movement.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Disable device-code flow where not needed; enforce Conditional Access and monitor Entra ID deviceCode sign-ins.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"BleepingComputer\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/tycoon2fa-hijacks-microsoft-365-accounts-via-device-code-phishing/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"eSentire\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.esentire.com/blog/tycoon-2fa-operators-adopt-oauth-device-code-phishing\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Proofpoint\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.proofpoint.com/us/blog/threat-insight/device-code-phishing-evolution-identity-takeover\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": false\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"[+24h Old] Russian hackers turn Kazuar backdoor into modular P2P botnet\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-16T10:15:00-04:00\",\n    \"summary\": \"Microsoft and others describe how the Russia-linked Secret Blizzard (overlapping Turla tradecraft) evolved Kazuar into a modular peer-to-peer botnet with leader election and proxying to reduce noisy outbound traffic. The architecture separates a kernel coordinator, a bridge communications layer, and worker modules for espionage functions like keylogging, file theft, and reconnaissance, using encrypted IPC to blend in.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Improves stealth and resilience for long-term espionage in government/diplomatic and critical environments. Harder-to-detect internal propagation increases dwell time risk.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Prioritize behavior-based detections and hunt for anomalous IPC + encrypted module traffic; segment and restrict outbound C2 paths.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"BleepingComputer\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/russian-hackers-turn-kazuar-backdoor-into-modular-p2p-botnet/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Microsoft Security Blog\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/05/14/kazuar-anatomy-of-a-nation-state-botnet/\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"[+24h Old] Popular node-ipc npm package compromised to steal credentials\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-15T13:10:00-04:00\",\n    \"summary\": \"Multiple security firms reported that new versions of node-ipc were published with obfuscated credential-stealing code that fingerprints hosts, collects cloud/dev secrets (AWS/Azure/GCP, SSH keys, CI/CD tokens, etc.), and exfiltrates via DNS TXT queries. Reporting indicates an external actor compromised an inactive maintainer account to publish malicious releases.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Supply-chain compromise can leak developer and infrastructure credentials, enabling downstream codebase breaches and cloud takeovers. DNS-based exfiltration can blend into normal traffic and evade naive controls.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Remove affected package versions immediately; rotate all potentially exposed secrets; review dependency lockfiles and CI build logs.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"BleepingComputer\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/popular-node-ipc-npm-package-compromised-to-steal-credentials/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Socket\",\n        \"url\": \"https://socket.dev/blog/node-ipc-package-compromised\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"OX Security\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.ox.security/blog/node-ipc-npm-package-infostealer-malware/\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"[+24h Old] Shai-Hulud worm steals npm, GitHub, AWS, and Kubernetes secrets from developers\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-15\",\n    \"summary\": \"Security reporting describes a self-propagating malware campaign called Shai-Hulud that targets developer workstations and pipelines to harvest tokens and secrets (npm publish tokens, GitHub tokens, cloud credentials, Kubernetes secrets) and then poisons packages to spread further. The campaign’s tooling was reportedly released publicly, accelerating copycat activity and broadening risk to the open-source ecosystem.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Threatens large-scale supply-chain propagation via poisoned packages and compromised developer identities. Stolen cloud and CI/CD secrets can enable rapid lateral movement into production.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Rotate developer and CI/CD secrets; enforce least-privilege tokens; enable package provenance controls and hardened GitHub Actions policies.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cyber Security News\",\n        \"url\": \"https://cybersecuritynews.com/shai-hulud-worm-steals-npm/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"SlowMist\",\n        \"url\": \"https://slowmist.medium.com/shai-hulud-malware-in-depth-analysis-open-source-means-loss-of-control-ca49cdc06bf7\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"[+24h Old] Gunra ransomware expands RaaS operations after shifting from Conti-based locker\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-15\",\n    \"summary\": \"Analysts detail how Gunra evolved from early Conti-derived tooling into a broader ransomware-as-a-service operation using underground forums for recruiting and operations. The reporting highlights a maturing affiliate ecosystem, white-label branding, and ongoing technical refinements across Windows and Linux builds.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Increases ransomware volume and diversification through affiliates and rebranding. Organizations may see new ransomware names that share common infrastructure and playbooks.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Harden perimeter access, monitor dark-web intelligence for early signals, and rehearse containment and recovery for double-extortion scenarios.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cyber Security News\",\n        \"url\": \"https://cybersecuritynews.com/gunra-ransomware-expands-raas-operations/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"S2W\",\n        \"url\": \"https://s2w.inc/en/resource/detail/1057\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"[+24h Old] Hackers abuse OAuth device authorization flow to steal Microsoft 365 tokens\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-15\",\n    \"summary\": \"Device-code phishing campaigns abuse OAuth’s device authorization flow by persuading victims to enter attacker-generated codes on legitimate Microsoft pages, yielding tokens that can persist beyond password changes. Reporting connects the growth of this technique to broad criminal adoption and difficulty of detection because the login happens on trusted domains.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Enables token-based account takeover at scale and facilitates BEC and internal data access without traditional phishing pages. Token persistence can prolong compromise windows.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Block/limit device-code flow, enforce compliant-device requirements, and add monitoring for device-code sign-ins and anomalous token use.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cyber Security News\",\n        \"url\": \"https://cybersecuritynews.com/hackers-abuse-oauth-device-authorization-flow/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Proofpoint\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.proofpoint.com/us/blog/threat-insight/device-code-phishing-evolution-identity-takeover\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"[+24h Old] OpenAI confirms limited internal impact from TanStack npm supply chain attack\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-15\",\n    \"summary\": \"OpenAI disclosed that two employee devices ingested malicious TanStack packages published via a compromised release pipeline, resulting in limited credential material exfiltration from internal repositories. As a precaution, OpenAI rotated credentials and revoked/re-signed code-signing certificates, with macOS users required to update apps by a set deadline to avoid trust failures.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Demonstrates downstream enterprise exposure from upstream open-source compromise; stolen credential material and signing keys can create high-leverage follow-on risk if misused.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Tighten software supply-chain controls (provenance, release gating, minimum package age) and accelerate endpoint and credential hardening for developer fleets.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"OpenAI\",\n        \"url\": \"https://openai.com/index/our-response-to-the-tanstack-npm-supply-chain-attack/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"SecurityWeek\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.securityweek.com/openai-hit-by-tanstack-supply-chain-attack/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cyber Security News\",\n        \"url\": \"https://cybersecuritynews.com/openai-confirms-security-breach/\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"[+24h Old] JDownloader website compromised to distribute malicious Windows and Linux installers\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-16\",\n    \"summary\": \"Reporting indicates attackers compromised the official JDownloader site and replaced certain installer links with trojanized Windows and Linux installers during a limited time window. Users were exposed to malware (including a Python-based RAT), while unaffected distribution channels included in-app updater paths and some package formats; remediation included taking the site offline and patching the vulnerable CMS.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Turns a trusted download source into a malware distribution point, potentially compromising large numbers of end users. Can lead to remote control, credential theft, and secondary payload deployment.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Re-download installers from verified sources, validate signatures/hashes, and scan endpoints that installed during the impacted window.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cyber Security News\",\n        \"url\": \"https://cybersecuritynews.com/jdownloader-website-compromised/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Malwarebytes\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.malwarebytes.com/blog/news/2026/05/attackers-replaced-jdownloader-installer-downloads-with-malware\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"[+24h Old] Pwn2Own Berlin 2026 Day 2 highlights: Exchange, Windows 11, Linux, and AI tools exploited\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-16\",\n    \"summary\": \"Day Two of Pwn2Own Berlin 2026 saw researchers demonstrate exploit chains across enterprise targets including Microsoft Exchange (multi-bug chain to SYSTEM-level impact), Windows 11 escalation, and Linux privilege escalation, alongside successful attacks on developer/AI tools. The disclosures provide vendors and defenders early-warning insight into realistic exploitation paths before patches land.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Shows feasible exploit chains against high-value enterprise infrastructure and emerging developer/AI tooling. Increases urgency for rapid patching once vendors ship fixes.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Track ZDI advisories and vendor patches; prioritize mitigation for core email and OS platforms; inventory exposure to exploited products.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cyber Security News\",\n        \"url\": \"https://cybersecuritynews.com/microsoft-exchange-windows-11-and-cursor-zero-days-exploited-pwn2own/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Zero Day Initiative\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.zerodayinitiative.com/blog/2026/5/15/pwn2own-berlin-2026-day-two-results\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": true\n  },\n  {\n    \"title\": \"[+24h Old] Funnel Builder WordPress plugin bug actively exploited to steal credit cards\",\n    \"published_at\": \"2026-05-15T15:30:00-04:00\",\n    \"summary\": \"A critical Funnel Builder (FunnelKit) plugin issue was observed under active exploitation, allowing attackers to inject malicious JavaScript into WooCommerce checkout flows via an exposed endpoint and plugin settings manipulation. The injected scripts acted as payment skimmers, exfiltrating card details and billing data from customers during checkout.\",\n    \"impact\": \"Direct financial fraud risk for e-commerce customers and merchants; can trigger PCI/brand damage and incident response costs. Compromised checkout pages can persist until cleaned.\",\n    \"recommendation\": \"Update to the fixed plugin release immediately and audit checkout “External Scripts” settings for injected code.\",\n    \"sources\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"BleepingComputer\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/funnel-builder-wordpress-plugin-bug-exploited-to-steal-credit-cards/\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Sansec\",\n        \"url\": \"https://sansec.io/research/funnelkit-woocommerce-vulnerability-exploited\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"is_older_than_24h\": true\n  }\n]